Below is a **complete “starter‑kit”** that shows how to pull a *reactive* Redis list (`RQueue`) called `taskQueue` while telling Redisson to serialize its elements with a custom JSON codec (`jsonCodec`).  
Everything is written in plain Java 11+ (you can drop into Kotlin/JVM projects without changes).

---

### 1️⃣  Dependencies  

```xml
<!-- Maven -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson-reactivex</artifactId>
    <version>3.x.y</version> <!-- pick latest stable release -->
</dependency>

<!-- If you’re using RxJava2 -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.reactivex.rxjava2</groupId>
    <artifactId>rxjava</artifactId>
    <version>2.2.21</version>
</dependency>
```

*(Replace `3.x.y` with the current Redisson version.)*

---

### 2️⃣  Custom JSON codec

Redisson ships with built‑in codecs such as `JacksonJSONCodec`.  
If you need something more exotic, simply implement `org.redisson.client.codec.Codec`.

```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import org.redisson.client.codec.Codec;
import org.redisson.client.protocol.Decoder;
import org.redisson.client.protocol.encoder.Encoder;

public class MyJsonCodec implements Codec {

    private static final ObjectMapper MAPPER = new ObjectMapper();

    @Override public Encoder getValueEncoder() {
        return value -> MAPPER.writeValueAsBytes(value);
    }

    @SuppressWarnings({"unchecked","rawtypes"})
    @Override public Decoder<?> getValueDecoder(Class clazz) {
        return bytes -> MAPPER.readValue(bytes, clazz);
    }
}
```

Instantiate once – it’s thread safe:

```java
static final Codec jsonCodec = new MyJsonCodec();
```

(If you prefer the out‑of‑the‑box `JacksonJSONCodec`, replace the above with  
`static final Codec jsonCodec = new JacksonJSONCodec();`.)

---

### 3️⃣  Building the **RedissonRxClient**

```java
import org.redisson.Redisson;
import org.redisson.api.RedissonRxClient;
import org.redisson.config.Config;

...

// ① Configure the cluster/server you connect to
Config cfg = new Config();
// e.g., single server mode
cfg.useSingleServer()
   .setAddress("redis://localhost:6379");

// Optional: set a global default serializer (not required here)
// cfg.setDefaultSerializer(new SomeOtherCodec());

// ② Create the normal RedissonClient first
RedissonClient syncClient = Redisson.create(cfg);

// ③ From the sync client ask for the Rx variant
RedissonRxClient rxClient = syncClient.rx();

// Now we’re ready to fetch our queue reactively!
```

---

### 4️⃣  Pulling the reactive queue with the chosen codec

```java
import org.redisson.api.RQueueReactive;
import io.reactivex.Flowable;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

...

String queueName = "taskQueue";

// ④ Get the reactive queue, passing the codec explicitly
RQueueReactive<MyJobClass> taskQueue =
        rxClient.<MyJobClass>getQueue(queueName, jsonCodec);

```

> ⚠️ **Why pass the codec here?**  
> With the RxAPI you can override the codec on a per‑key basis—useful when different queues store different data shapes.

---

### 5️⃣  Using the queue reactively

Here are some quick examples showing typical CRUD patterns:

| Operation | Code |
|-----------|------|
| **Add a job** | ```java<br/>Flowable.fromPublisher(taskQueue.offer(myJob))<br/>          .timeout(500, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)<br/>          .blockingSubscribe(success -> System.out.println(\"Enqueued\"));<br/>``` |
| **Peek next job** | ```java<br/>taskQueue.peek()<br/>      .subscribe(job -> System.out.println(\"Next job:\": +job), Throwable::printStackTrace);<br/>``` |
| **Pop & process jobs continuously** | ```java<br/>taskQueue.poll(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)<br/>      .repeatWhen(completed -> completed.delay(100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS))<br/>      .filter(java.util.Objects::nonNull)<br/>      .map(this::processJob)<br/>      .subscribe(result -> {}, Throwable::printStackTrace);<br/>``` |

> 👉 All calls return **RxJava2 Publishers** (`Observable`, `Maybe`, `Completable`, …).  
> You can switch to **Reactor** (`Flux`, `Mono`) if you prefer that ecosystem – the pattern stays identical.

---

### 6️⃣  Full minimal runnable demo

```java
package com.example.demo;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonCreator;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonProperty;
import io.reactivex.Flowable;
import org.redisson.Redisson;
import org.redisson.api.*;
import org.redisson.api.redisnode.RedisNode;
import org.redisson.config.Config;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

/**
 * Demo: Reactive Queue with custom JSON codec
 */
public class TaskQueueDemo {

    /* ---------- 1️⃣ Custom JSON codec ----------------------------------- */

    public record Job(String id, String payload) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        /* ---------- 2️⃣ Setup --------------------------------------------------*/
        Config cfg = new Config();
        cfg.useSingleServer().setAddress("redis://localhost:6379");

        RedissonClient sync = Redisson.create(cfg);
        RedissonRxClient rx = sync.rx();

        /* ---------- 3️⃣ Obtain the reactive queue ----------------------------*/
        String qName = "taskQueue";
        RQueueReactive<Job> queue = rx.<Job>getQueue(qName, new JsonJacksonCodec());

        /* ---------- 4️⃣ Push a few test jobs --------------------------------*/
        List<Job> initialJobs = List.of(
                new Job("a1", "{\"msg\":\"hello\"}"),
                new Job("b2", "{\"msg\":\"world\"}")
        );

        Flowable.fromIterable(initialJobs)
                .flatMap(j -> queue.offer(j))
                .ignoreElements()
                .blockingAwait();

        /* ---------- 5️⃣ Consume them reactively -----------------------------*/
        queue.take(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
             .takeUntil(item -> item.id.equals("stop")) // stop after special marker
             .doOnComplete(() -> System.out.println("\nAll done!"))
             .subscribe(
                     j -> System.out.printf("[Consumed] %s%n", j),
                     err -> { throw new RuntimeException(err); });

        /* ---------- 6️⃣ Clean shutdown -------------------------------------*/
        Thread.sleep(2000); // let everything finish
        sync.shutdown();
    }
}

class JsonJacksonCodec extends org.redisson.codec.JacksonJSONCodec {}
```

Run the program → you’ll see two jobs printed, followed by the completion signal.

---

#### TL;DR

1. **Create a `RedissonRxClient`.**  
2. **Call `.getQueue(<name>, <yourCustomCodec>)`.**  
3. **Use the returned `RQueueReactive<T>` exactly like any other RxJava publisher.**

That’s all you need to wire a reactive queue backed by Redis with a bespoke JSON encoder/decoder. Happy coding!
